//! Golden vectors proving sdroxide's `rade_text` port is interoperable with
//! FreeDV GUI, which is the whole point of the codec: a callsign we transmit
//! has to be the one another FreeDV station decodes, and vice versa.
//!
//! Generated by compiling the upstream C++ reference — `rade_text.cpp`,
//! `ldpc_encode.cpp`, `ldpc_decode.cpp` and `HRA_56_56.h` from
//! <https://github.com/tmiw/freedv-backend> at commit
//! `a096157a982f813e1600ab3b656708090191508e` (BSD-2-Clause) — against a small driver that
//! printed its output, which was pasted here. Regenerate the same way if
//! upstream ever changes the waveform.
//!
//! Two different standards of proof, deliberately:
//!
//! * **Transmit is compared exactly.** The encoder only ever emits `-1.0`,
//!   `0.0` and `1.0`, all exactly representable, so bit-for-bit equality is a
//!   legitimate assertion rather than a flaky one.
//! * **Receive is compared at the decision level** — the recovered callsign,
//!   not the intermediate LLRs. The decoder runs `exp`/`ln` in `f64`, whose
//!   last-ulp behaviour differs between libm implementations, so matching
//!   intermediates across toolchains is not something to promise. The sigmas
//!   below sit well inside (0.0–0.4) and well outside (1.0) the code's
//!   waterfall; a disagreement at a marginal sigma near 0.6 would be expected
//!   numerical divergence, not a bug.

